Prevalence and the normalization mechanism, configuration ariel, pipeline norm_xgb

1. Precision at realistic base rates. The grid is balanced by construction, so every
   accuracy in the paper assumes a 50 % prevalence. Recall and specificity are properties
   of the classifier and do not change with prevalence; precision does.

   threshold  recall  specif.    π=0.5    π=0.2    π=0.1   π=0.05   π=0.01
       0.500   98.0%    95.0%    95.1%    82.9%    68.4%    50.6%    16.4%
       0.215   99.4%    91.6%    92.2%    74.8%    56.8%    38.4%    10.7%
       0.900   91.6%    98.3%    98.2%    93.2%    86.0%    74.4%    35.7%
       0.990   74.2%    99.6%    99.5%    98.0%    95.6%    91.2%    66.5%

   at π = 0.10, 50 % precision needs threshold 0.090 and retains 99.8 % recall
   at π = 0.05, 50 % precision needs threshold 0.519 and retains 97.8 % recall
   at π = 0.01, 50 % precision needs threshold 0.981 and retains 80.8 % recall

2. Why per-spectrum normalization helps: the absolute-depth nuisance direction.
   Raw vs normalized features trained and tested on radius-restricted populations,
   training-set size held fixed at 4,000 planets per band.

    radius (R_E)      raw   normalized      gap   depth span
            1-26   80.90%       96.10%  +15.19      4.49 dex
             1-6   74.18%       94.60%  +20.43      4.24 dex
            6-12   84.62%       95.27%  +10.65      2.89 dex
           12-20   87.44%       96.17%   +8.74      2.33 dex

   The normalization advantage falls from +15.2 points on the full grid to
   +8.7 on the narrowest band, tracking the shrinking spread of absolute transit depth.
